# threads.topic -- Botlish-owned threads and actors across them
# (BOTLISH-OWNED-THREADS-AND-CROSS-THREAD-ACTORS-MILESTONE-1.md): the
# concepts and their diagnostics, one topic per code.

@topic concept.threads
title: Threads: isolated VMs started with std::thread
summary: A module's `thread fn` is a thread entry; std::thread::start runs it on an OS thread in a VM of its own, with a copied initialization value; join copies its result back.
index: concepts
alias: thread; start a thread; spawn a thread; worker thread; run in parallel; thread fn; join a thread; background thread
keywords: thread, std::thread, thread fn, entry, start, join, await_end, request_stop, stop_requested, Threads, ThreadLimit, ThreadFailed, ThreadStartFailed, VM, isolation, guard, NATIVE LIMIT STACK, parallel
related: concept.remote-actors concept.sendability concept.contexts concept.actors stdlib.module.std::thread error.thread-entry-placement error.thread-entry-signature error.thread-entry-environment error.thread-entry-reference error.context-trait-selection-conflict error.thread-unavailable capability.concurrency

== WHAT IT IS
A *thread entry* is a top-level function of a module declared `thread fn
NAME(init: I) -> R`. `std::thread::start(std::thread::entry(NAME), init)`
starts an OS thread running it: the runtime makes a new VM of the same
compiled program on that thread -- its own heap and collector, its own
module statics, its own installed contexts, its own native stack and
stack-overflow guard -- copies `init` into it (std::transfer: I and R are
sendable types), runs the entry, and keeps its result for
`std::thread::join`, which copies it back. Nothing of one VM's heap is ever
visible to another: threads share no value, only bytes the runtime copies.

== RULES
  * Starting needs the installed context std::thread::Threads
    (`std::thread::create(LIMIT)`): the authority to start at most LIMIT
    threads at once. Restricted injection withholds it.
  * A worker has none of its creator's contexts. An entry installs what it
    needs with `with context EXPR` statements of its own body; the
    program's selection of each context trait is the same for every VM
    (error.context-trait-selection-conflict).
  * start gives a Started value: `thread`, the affine join right
    (Thread[R]: join collects the outcome once), and `id`, an unrestricted
    ThreadId (status, request_stop, a bounded await_end).
  * join answers the entry's value, or fails with ThreadFailed (the
    entry's error identity and message) or ThreadStartFailed.
  * Stopping is cooperative: request_stop sets a flag the thread reads
    (`std::thread::stop_requested?()`) and wakes its port; nothing kills a
    thread. When the main VM completes, every thread still running is asked
    to stop and joined.
  * At most 64 threads run guarded in a process (the main one's included):
    a start beyond is ThreadLimit, never an unguarded thread. A native
    stack overflow on any thread ends the process (NATIVE LIMIT STACK).
  * Native backends only: interp and compile refuse a program that starts
    a thread (error.thread-unavailable).

@example accept
import worker
import std::thread

fn run(context t: std::thread::Threads) -> int errors std::thread::ThreadLimit, std::thread::ThreadStartFailed, std::thread::ThreadFailed:
    {thread} = std::thread::start(std::thread::entry(worker::crunch), 20)
    std::thread::join(thread)

with context std::thread::create(4)
run():
    on std::thread::ThreadLimit:
        -1
    on std::thread::ThreadStartFailed {reason}:
        -2
    on std::thread::ThreadFailed {identity, message}:
        -3
@module worker
fn fib(n: int) -> int:
    if n < 2:
        n
    else:
        fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2)

thread fn crunch(n: int) -> int:
    fib(n)
@caption fib(20) computed by another thread, in a VM of its own
@backends cranelift
@value 6765
@end

@topic concept.remote-actors
title: Actors across threads: std::actor::remote
summary: std::actor::remote exports a mailbox as a sendable Remote[M] that other VMs send to, and makes typed calls between VMs; messages are copied bytes and a full endpoint waits for room.
index: concepts
alias: remote actor; cross-thread actor; message another thread; send to another thread; channel between threads; request reply between threads; actor on another thread
keywords: std::actor::remote, Remote, Service, Reply, Caller, export, send, deliver, serve_calls, call, reply, CallFailed, Full, Closed, Stale, TypeMismatch, Undelivered, ServiceEnded, port, std::thread::serve, backpressure
related: concept.threads concept.actors concept.sendability stdlib.module.std::actor::remote stdlib.module.std::thread

== WHAT IT IS
An actor's mailbox stays local to its VM (an std::actor::Address is not
sendable). `std::actor::remote::export(address, CAPACITY)` gives it an
endpoint in the process's transport and answers a Remote[M]: a sendable
capability -- put it in a thread's initialization value or a message -- to
send M values into that mailbox from any VM. `send` answers at once
(Delivered, Full, Closed, Stale, TooLarge, TypeMismatch); `deliver`, from
an event-loop task, waits for room instead. Messages from one sender
arrive in order.

A service is a mailbox of Call[Q, R] values exported with serve_calls:
each call carries its request and a Reply[R]. An actor calls a service
with `call(service, q, caller)` and is suspended -- its thread is not --
until the reply arrives or the call fails (CallFailed with the reason:
Undelivered when the service closed with the request queued, ServiceEnded
when its VM ended owing the answer). No call is left waiting because its
service went.

== RULES
  * Each VM that moves remote messages installs its port
    (`with context std::thread::port()`) and serves it once on its event
    loop (`std::thread::serve()`, with the Linux driver's
    event_loop_linux::Native, which implements std::thread::Doorbell).
  * A capability names its endpoint by a random key and a generation: one
    nobody was given cannot be made (an opaque struct), a closed endpoint's
    is Closed, a reused slot's Stale.
  * The transport checks each message's schema against the endpoint's: a
    capability retyped through a copy is TypeMismatch, never a delivery.
  * Capabilities carry authority to send, never to receive or act; a
    worker that needs an operation of its creator's context gets a service
    (a broker context of its own holding it), never the context.

@topic error.thread-entry-placement
title: THREAD-ENTRY-PLACEMENT: a thread entry that is no module's top level
summary: `thread fn` is declared at the top level of a module, never in the entry program (whose top level no worker runs) and never nested in another function.
index: errors
alias: thread fn in main program; thread fn nested
keywords: thread fn, entry program, module, top level, nested
related: concept.threads error.thread-entry-signature
code: CORE SEMANTIC THREAD-ENTRY-PLACEMENT

== MEANING
A worker VM initializes the program's modules and runs one entry; it never
runs the entry program's top level, which installs the main VM's contexts
and does its work. So an entry lives in a module, where everything it
reaches is module code every VM initializes for itself.

== WHAT TO DO
Move the `thread fn` into a module the entry program imports.

@example reject
thread fn here(n: int) -> int:
    n

1
@caption A thread entry in the entry program
@error CORE SEMANTIC THREAD-ENTRY-PLACEMENT
@end

@topic error.thread-entry-signature
title: THREAD-ENTRY-SIGNATURE: a thread entry's parameter or result
summary: A thread entry has exactly one declared, sendable, concrete parameter (its initialization value) and a declared sendable result; no context parameters, flags, places or resume clause.
index: errors
alias: thread fn parameters; thread fn context parameter; thread fn not sendable
keywords: thread fn, parameter, result, sendable, context parameter, initialization value
related: concept.threads concept.sendability error.not-sendable
code: CORE SEMANTIC THREAD-ENTRY-SIGNATURE

== MEANING
The initialization value is copied into the new VM and the result copied
back (std::transfer), so both are declared sendable types; a struct holds
several values. A context parameter would be a context of the creator's,
which belongs to another VM: an entry installs its own contexts in its
body instead.

== WHAT TO DO
Gather what the thread needs into one struct of sendable fields; declare
the result type (`-> unit` for none); install contexts with `with context`
in the entry's body.

@example reject
import worker

1
@module worker
thread fn add(a: int, b: int) -> int:
    a + b
@caption Two parameters: put them in a struct
@error CORE SEMANTIC THREAD-ENTRY-SIGNATURE
@end

@topic error.thread-entry-environment
title: THREAD-ENTRY-ENVIRONMENT: a thread entry that captures or suspends
summary: A thread entry is environment-free and runs to its end on its own thread's stack: it captures no value and never suspends (its coroutines are constructed in its body).
index: errors
alias: thread fn yields; thread fn captures
keywords: thread fn, capture, yield, suspend, environment
related: concept.threads concept.coroutines
code: CORE SEMANTIC THREAD-ENTRY-ENVIRONMENT

== MEANING
A worker's VM shares nothing with its creator's, so an entry cannot refer
to a value of another function's scope, and it is no coroutine body: a
thread runs its entry once, to completion.

== WHAT TO DO
Pass what the entry needs in its initialization value; construct
coroutines inside the entry and resume them there.

@topic error.thread-entry-reference
title: THREAD-ENTRY-REFERENCE: a thread entry used as a function
summary: A thread entry is named only as std::thread::entry's argument; it is never called directly or taken as a value, and std::thread::entry takes only a thread entry.
index: errors
alias: call a thread fn; thread fn as value; std::thread::entry of a function
keywords: thread fn, std::thread::entry, call, function value
related: concept.threads error.thread-entry-placement
code: CORE SEMANTIC THREAD-ENTRY-REFERENCE

== MEANING
An entry runs in a VM of its own, made for it by std::thread::start;
calling it in the current VM, or holding it as a function value, would run
it with the wrong VM's contexts and heap.

== WHAT TO DO
Start it: `std::thread::start(std::thread::entry(NAME), init)`. To run
the same code in this VM, move it into an ordinary function the entry
calls.

@example reject
import worker

worker::crunch(3)
@module worker
thread fn crunch(n: int) -> int:
    n * n
@caption A thread entry called directly
@error CORE SEMANTIC THREAD-ENTRY-REFERENCE
@end

@topic error.context-trait-selection-conflict
title: CONTEXT-TRAIT-SELECTION-CONFLICT: two VMs select different implementations
summary: The program selects one installed implementation of each context trait for every VM: the entry program and the thread entries that install one must install the same.
index: errors
alias: different drivers in threads; context trait per thread
keywords: context trait, selection, thread entry, driver, implementation, monomorphization
related: concept.threads concept.context-traits error.ambiguous-context-implementation
code: CORE SEMANTIC CONTEXT-TRAIT-SELECTION-CONFLICT

== MEANING
A context trait's operations compile to direct calls of the one installed
implementation, selected for the whole program (there is no run-time
dispatch). Every VM runs the same compiled image, so a thread entry that
installs another implementation than the entry program (or another entry)
would need code the program does not have.

== WHAT TO DO
Install the same implementation in every VM that installs one (for
std::event_loop::Driver: the same driver), or move the differing work into
another program.

@topic error.thread-unavailable
title: THREAD-UNAVAILABLE: threads on the Tcl backends
summary: interp and compile refuse a program that starts a Botlish thread before it runs, and std::thread's runtime operations when reached: they have no OS threads, each running a VM.
index: errors backends
alias: threads on interp; thread on compile backend
keywords: interp, compile, thread, std::thread, native, backend
related: concept.threads backend.native-only error.native-only
code: BACKEND THREAD-UNAVAILABLE
code: CORE SEMANTIC THREAD-UNAVAILABLE

== MEANING
Not an error of the program, but of the backend chosen for it. The Tcl
backends run one interpreter per process, without OS threads; they never
emulate threads. A program that names a thread entry
(std::thread::entry) is refused before it runs (BACKEND
THREAD-UNAVAILABLE); a program compiled for them that reaches one of the
runtime's thread or transport operations anyway is refused there (CORE
SEMANTIC THREAD-UNAVAILABLE).

== WHAT TO DO
Run the program on a native backend: `-backend cranelift`, or build an
executable.
